/* ============================================================================
 * FeatherThermometer.ino
 *
 * Battery-powered BLE thermometer. Advertises live readings as a beacon, and
 * accepts a GATT connection for reliable in-order delivery. The client picks
 * whichever it wants.
 *
 * PARTS
 *   Adafruit 5323  ESP32-S3 Feather, 8MB flash / no PSRAM, STEMMA QT
 *                  (MAX17048 fuel gauge on board, I2C 0x36, post-Feb-2023 rev)
 *   Adafruit 4650  128x64 monochrome OLED FeatherWing, SH1107, I2C 0x3C
 *   Adafruit 642   High-temp waterproof DS18B20 1-Wire probe (+4.7k resistor)
 *   Adafruit 481   16mm rugged metal momentary pushbutton w/ blue LED ring
 *   Adafruit 258   3.7V 1200mAh LiPo, JST-PH
 *
 * BEHAVIOUR
 *   - While on, a long press (>=1200 ms) turns the unit off. "Off" is ESP32
 *     deep sleep; the button is the EXT0 wake source.
 *   - While off, a long press of the same duration turns it on. Symmetric
 *     hold at both ends, for the same reason bike lights do it: a knock in a
 *     bag must not switch the unit on. A brief press wakes the chip, fails
 *     the hold check and puts it straight back to sleep, having initialised
 *     nothing. The press that starts it is then swallowed, so it counts as
 *     neither a short press nor the start of a long press.
 *   - Power-on and power-off are immediate; there is no splash screen. The
 *     digit area stays blank until the first conversion lands (~750 ms); the
 *     battery icon is what tells you the unit is on.
 *   - Battery state is a permanent icon in the top right corner rather than
 *     a screen of its own, and a Bluetooth icon appears beside it while a
 *     central is connected.
 *   - While on, the temperature is redrawn every time the DS18B20 delivers a
 *     new conversion (~1.3 Hz at 12-bit resolution).
 *   - Every sample carries a monotonic sequence number.
 *   - Not connected: a connectable advertisement carries the latest sample in
 *     manufacturer data, so a passive scanner gets live values.
 *   - Connected: the same sample goes out as a notification. The BLE Link
 *     Layer acknowledges and retransmits, and ATT preserves ordering, so
 *     nothing is lost or reordered for as long as the link is up.
 *   - A short press does not change power state; it increments a press
 *     counter carried in both the beacon and the notification.
 *
 *   Nothing is buffered. Samples taken while no central is connected are not
 *   retained, by design - the client is expected to connect when it wants a
 *   continuous record and to accept gaps when it does not.
 *
 * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * WIRING
 * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
 *
 * OLED FeatherWing (4650)
 *   Stacks straight onto the Feather headers - no discrete wiring. For
 *   reference, the wing uses:
 *       3V, GND
 *       SDA  = GPIO3    (5k pull-up on the Feather, fed from the I2C power pin)
 *       SCL  = GPIO4    (5k pull-up on the Feather)
 *       RST  = Feather RST (the wing's reset button)
 *       A/B/C buttons = GPIO9 / GPIO6 / GPIO5   <-- NOT used by this sketch,
 *                                                   but keep those pins free.
 *
 * DS18B20 probe (642)
 *   The probe ships in one of two colour schemes - check yours before wiring:
 *       Scheme 1:  Red          -> 3V
 *                  Black        -> GND
 *                  White        -> DATA
 *       Scheme 2:  Orange strip -> 3V
 *                  White        -> GND
 *                  Blue strip   -> DATA
 *   DATA -> A1 (GPIO17)
 *   4.7k resistor from A1 (GPIO17) to 3V.  This pull-up is mandatory; the
 *   bus does not work without it. Parasite power is not used.
 *
 * Pushbutton (481) - switch section (3 spade terminals)
 *   C  (common)          -> GND
 *   NO (normally open)   -> A0 (GPIO18)
 *   NC                   -> not connected
 *   The internal pull-up on GPIO18 is enabled in software, so pressed = LOW.
 *   GPIO18 is an RTC GPIO, which is what makes EXT0 deep-sleep wake possible.
 *
 * Pushbutton (481) - LED ring (2 spade terminals), OPTIONAL
 *   The ring is rated 3-6 V and has its own series resistor. It is too much
 *   load for a GPIO, and at 3.3 V it is dim, so drive it low-side off the
 *   battery rail:
 *       LED+            -> BAT   (3.7-4.2 V from the LiPo)
 *       LED-            -> collector of an NPN (2N3904 / BC337 / MMBT3904)
 *       NPN emitter     -> GND
 *       NPN base        -> 1k -> A2 (GPIO16)
 *   Set USE_BUTTON_LED to 0 below if you are not fitting the transistor.
 *
 * Battery (258)
 *   Plug into the Feather's JST-PH connector. The MAX17048 needs the battery
 *   present to report a meaningful percentage - on USB-only the reading is
 *   not trustworthy.
 *
 * ===========================================================================
 * BLE INTERFACE
 * ===========================================================================
 *
 * All multi-byte fields are LITTLE ENDIAN.
 *
 * Temperature encoding, used everywhere:
 *   int16, units of 0.01 degC.  21.5 degC -> 2150.  -3.2 degC -> -320.
 *   0x8000 (-32768) means "no valid reading".
 *
 * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * Advertisement (connectable, undirected; stops while a central is connected)
 * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
 *   Flags, short local name, 16-bit service UUID 0x181A, and AD type 0xFF
 *   Manufacturer Specific Data, 8 bytes:
 *
 *     [0..1]  Company Identifier. 0xFFFF is the SIG value reserved for
 *             internal/test use - replace it with your own assigned ID.
 *     [2..3]  Sequence number, uint16.
 *     [4..5]  Temperature, int16.
 *     [6]     Battery percentage, uint8, 0..100.
 *     [7]     Press count, uint8, wraps at 256.
 *
 *   The sequence number matters most in the beacon, where there is no
 *   delivery guarantee: without it a receiver cannot tell a fresh sample that
 *   happens to read the same as the last one from a repeat of the same
 *   advertisement. Compare against the previous value to deduplicate and to
 *   count how many samples were missed. Same for the press count: the
 *   difference since the last observation is the number of presses, which
 *   survives an arbitrarily long scan gap and copes with two quick presses.
 *   Over a connection both are simply confirmation that nothing was dropped.
 *
 *   Sequence numbers persist across deep sleep, so switching the unit off and
 *   on does not reset them. They wrap at 65536, about 13.6 hours of
 *   continuous running at 750 ms per sample. They do reset to zero on a cold
 *   boot with no RTC retention, e.g. after the battery is disconnected, so a
 *   client should treat a large backwards jump as a restart rather than a
 *   wrap.
 *
 * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * Standard services (so generic BLE apps work without knowing anything)
 * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
 *   0x181A  Environmental Sensing
 *     0x2A6E  Temperature          read, notify   int16, 0.01 degC
 *   0x180F  Battery Service
 *     0x2A19  Battery Level        read, notify   uint8, 0..100
 *
 * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * Custom service  b7e1c0a0-4f3d-4a21-9f3e-6c1a2d5b7e01
 * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
 *
 * MEASUREMENT  ...7e02   read, notify   8 bytes, one per sample
 *     [0..1]  Sequence number, uint16
 *     [2..3]  Temperature, int16
 *     [4]     Battery percentage, uint8
 *     [5]     Press count, uint8
 *     [6]     Flags: bit0 = probe present
 *                    bit1 = button currently held
 *     [7]     Reserved, 0
 *
 *   This exists alongside 0x2A6E because it delivers the whole sample as one
 *   atomic packet. Reading temperature and battery from two characteristics
 *   cannot tell you they came from the same instant.
 *
 * PLANNED, NOT YET IMPLEMENTED
 *   CALIBRATION  ...7e06   read, write   8 bytes
 *     float scale, float offset, little endian, applied as
 *     corrected = raw * scale + offset.
 *   Calibration is an app feature in the shipping product: the app walks the
 *   user through an ice-slush and a boiling-water measurement, asks for
 *   station pressure to get the true boiling point, computes the pair and
 *   writes it here, and the Feather persists it in NVS. This MVP hard codes
 *   the pair for one specific probe instead - see the calibration block
 *   further down.
 *
 * TYPICAL CLIENT FLOW
 *   Scan for the name or 0x181A and read the beacon for a live value, or
 *   connect and subscribe to MEASUREMENT for an unbroken stream. Timestamp
 *   notifications on arrival; the device has no clock of its own.
 *
 * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * EMBEDDED FONT
 * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * The digits are Spleen 32x64 and Spleen 16x32, version 2.2.0. Only the
 * twelve characters this sketch needs are embedded: '0'-'9', '-' and '.'.
 *
 *   Spleen
 *   Copyright (c) 2018-2026, Frederic Cambus
 *   https://www.cambus.net/
